{"year":"2026","sessionID":"2026GS","sessionIndex":"1","billNumber":"HB0330","billNumberShort":"H.B. 330","billNumberLong":"HB0330S02","fileNumber":"2026FL1195","shortTitle":"Liability Limitations Amendments","currentSubVersion":"2","currentMinVersion":"0","primeSponsor":"JACKC","primeSponsorName":"Rep. Jack, Colin W.","primeSponsorHouse":"H","floorSponsor":"WINTER","floorSponsorName":"Sen. Winterton, Ronald M.","floorSponsorHouse":"S","generalProvisions":"This bill creates an affirmative defense.","highlightedProvisions":"This bill:<hr><ltbullet>creates an affirmative defense in a civil action for conduct, an omission, or a condition authorized or required by a governmental entity;<hr><ltbullet>specifies the circumstances in which the affirmative defense described above does not apply, including in an action for products liability;<hr><ltbullet>clarifies when conduct, an omission, or a condition is considered to have occurred for purposes of the affirmative defense;<hr><ltbullet>clarifies that lawful authorization to engage in an activity does not eliminate a duty to exercise reasonable care; <hr><ltbullet>specifies that the affirmative defense applies regardless of the theory of liability; and<hr><ltbullet>abrogates the common law to the extent the common law is inconsistent with the affirmative defense.","moniesAppropriated":"None","draftingAttorney":"Michael Cipriano","fiscalAnalyst":"Gary R. Syphus","lastAction":"Governor Signed","lastActionOwner":"Lieutenant Governor's office for filing","lastActionDate":"3/18/2026","trackingID":"FVGJYDRRBR","actionHistoryList":[{"description":"Bill Numbered but not Distributed","owner":"Legislative Research and General Counsel","actionDate":"2026-01-22 13:50:38.443","actionClass":"L","voteID":"","voiceVote":"","voteHouse":"","voteStr":"","actionCode":"LNUMBER","parm1":"","parm2":""},{"description":"Numbered Bill Publicly Distributed","owner":"Legislative Research and General Counsel","actionDate":"2026-01-22 13:50:38.443","ac